  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A cold front will cross through the area\nThursday into Thursday night bringing the potential for a round\nof showers and storms.\n\nHigh pressure that has been over the region will shift offshore \ntonight. In the mid and upper levels, ridging will begin to \nbreakdown with height falls through tomorrow as upper-level \ntroughing approaches. A weakening piece of upper-level energy \napproaches the region tonight which will be associated with decaying \nshowers and thunderstorms from off to our northwest. A more \npronounced shortwave will pass through the region Thursday into \nThursday night, with a cold front tracking from northwest to \nsoutheast across the region during the evening into overnight hours. \n\nSome showers and a few embedded storms are expected tonight into \nearly Thursday morning for mainly the Lehigh Valley, Poconos, and \nadjacent portions of northern NJ as the decaying line of convection \noriginating from the eastern Great Lakes will be tracking toward the \narea. There is some potential for this activity to make it all the \nway to the I-95 corridor by around daybreak Thursday. PoPs during \nthis timeframe are generally 50-60% across the Poconos, 20-40% for \nthe Lehigh Valley and far northern NJ, and around 20% along I-95. \nElsewhere, conditions will likely remain dry. \n\nOn Thursday, the cold front will begin to approach the region by the \nafternoon and progress through the area into Thursday night. The \nstrongest shear (0-6 km Bulk Shear exceeding 40 kts) looks to remain \nacross northern parts of the area and weaken with southern extent to \naround 20 kts. Meanwhile, destabilization looks modest at best and \nconfined to southern parts of the area. Given these factors, the \nmost likely scenario is scattered showers and storms with a very\nlimited severe threat. However, some limited hydro concerns \ncould evolve Thursday afternoon into Thursday night across \nmainly Delmarva into southeastern PA and southern NJ as PWATs \nreach upwards of 2 inches and there is warm cloud layer \nprocesses allowing for efficient rainfall. WPC has a Marginal (1\nof 4) Excessive Rainfall Outlook over these areas. Showers and \nstorms look to clear southern parts of the forecast area by \nFriday morning. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Another disturbance will approach the area on \nSaturday night into Sunday and produce a round of showers and \nthunderstorms.\n\nBehind Thursday's front, a drier and more comfortable airmass will \nsettle into the region on Friday, and into at least the start of \nSaturday, as high pressure temporarily settles in. \n\nThis quick shot of high pressure will push east later in the day on \nSaturday. This will allow Thursday's cold front to push back north \nas a warm front, and it will likely be have a weak low with it. \nTiming adjustments are likely over the next few days, but as of this \nafternoon, it will be unsettled Saturday afternoon into Sunday. At \nthis point, the highest chances for showers and thunderstorms are on \nSaturday night with PoPs in the 60-70% range areawide.\n\nThe front and associated precipitation look to clear the area late \non Sunday, with warm and dry conditions to start next work week.",
